   {
    "n": "06",
    "title": "Sayings, commands and the long road home",
    "blocks": [
     {
      "k": "h3",
      "t": "A proverb, not a guarantee"
     },
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       "Here is the verse parents quote most:"
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "quote",
      "r": "PRO.22.6",
      "label": "Proverbs 22:6",
      "kind": "saying",
      "why": null
     },
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       "It is a proverb, a short saying about how life usually goes, and Proverbs shows how its sayings work. ",
       {
        "r": "PRO.26.4",
        "t": "Proverbs 26:4"
       },
       " tells you not to answer a fool according to his folly, and ",
       {
        "r": "PRO.26.5",
        "t": "26:5"
       },
       " tells you to answer him. Both are true, in different situations, and wisdom knows which one fits. So ",
       {
        "r": "PRO.22.6",
        "t": "Proverbs 22:6"
       },
       " describes the usual fruit of careful training. It is not God’s promise that every child so trained will believe."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "open",
      "runs": [
       "The Hebrew words behind “in the way he should go” are, word for word, “according to his way”. Readers take them in more than one way: the right way a child ought to go; a way suited to this particular child’s age and nature; or, a few hold, the child’s own chosen way, as a warning ",
       {
        "c": "named commentators who hold each reading, read directly"
       },
       ". This study does not choose. All three readings leave the verse a saying about how things tend to go. Read more in ",
       {
        "s": "../the-wisdom-books-directions/",
        "t": "The wisdom books"
       },
       "."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "h3",
      "t": "Commands to children, and to fathers"
     },
     {
      "k": "quote",
      "r": "EPH.6.1-4",
      "label": "Ephesians 6:1–4",
      "kind": "command",
      "why": null
     },
     {
      "k": "quote",
      "r": "COL.3.21",
      "label": "Colossians 3:21",
      "kind": "command",
      "why": null
     },
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       "The children are spoken to directly, as people who can hear and answer. In the same breath the fathers are told not to provoke them, and Colossians gives a reason a parent can feel: “so they will not become discouraged”. Authority in these verses is given for the child’s good. It is never permission to frighten or crush. The confession this site writes under speaks of leading children “through consistent lifestyle example and loving discipline” (article XVIII, the family) ",
       {
        "c": "wording against bfm.sbc.net"
       },
       ". Read more in ",
       {
        "s": "../faith-in-daily-life-directions/",
        "t": "Faith in daily life"
       },
       ", and on the commandment itself in ",
       {
        "s": "../the-ten-commandments-directions/",
        "t": "The Ten Commandments"
       },
       "."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "care",
      "runs": [
       "A study on parents’ authority will be read by children whose homes are not safe. God never commands a child to stay in danger. Ephesians says “in the Lord”: no parent’s word can require what the Lord forbids, and harming a child is forbidden. Getting to safety and telling someone does not disobey these verses. That last sentence is this site’s application, carried from ",
       {
        "s": "../faith-in-daily-life-directions/",
        "t": "Faith in daily life"
       },
       ", and it is not a quotation from Paul. The help lines are at the top of this page."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "h3",
      "t": "The father who watched the road"
     },
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       "Jesus told a story, a parable, about a younger son who demanded his inheritance, wasted it in a far country, and came home with nothing."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "quote",
      "r": "LUK.15.20",
      "label": "Luke 15:20",
      "kind": "story",
      "why": null
     },
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       "“While he was still in the distance, his father saw him.” He was looking. The parable is first about God and the sinner who comes home to him, and that is its main point. Read more in ",
       {
        "s": "../the-good-news-directions/",
        "t": "The good news: how a person is made right with God"
       },
       ". But a parent who waits for a wandering child may notice what the father did and did not do. He did not drag his son back from the far country. He did not stop watching the road. When the son came, he ran. These observations are this site’s application, not a parenting rule Jesus gave."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "limits",
      "runs": [
       "The parable does not promise that every wandering child comes home, and this study will not promise it either."
      ]
     }
    ],
    "readings": [
     "PRO.22.6",
     "EPH.6.1-4",
     "COL.3.20-21",
     "LUK.15.20"
    ],
    "readingsNote": "each quoted here and walked in its own study",
    "hook": [
     "A saying is not a contract. A father keeps watching the road."
    ],
    "consider": [
     "What kind of sentence is ",
     {
      "r": "PRO.22.6",
      "t": "Proverbs 22:6"
     },
     ", and how does ",
     {
      "r": "PRO.26.4-5",
      "t": "Proverbs 26:4–5"
     },
     " show that kind of sentence working? What do the commands to fathers add to the commands to children?"
    ],
    "reflect": [
     "If a child of yours is far away, in distance or in faith, what would it look like this week to keep watching the road?"
    ]
   }
