  "title": "Children and the home",
  "concepts": [
   "Doorposts",
   "Generations",
   "Favourites",
   "Waiting"
  ],
  "motto": [
   "A doorpost. A table. A road home.",
   "Words carried from one generation to the next."
  ],
  "lead": [
   "The Bible says a great deal about children and the people who bring them up. It says it in very different kinds of sentence: commands, songs, stories and short sayings. This study walks the passages it owns and teaches one habit for every line about family: before you hold a verse as a promise, ask what kind of sentence it is."
  ],
  "frame": [
   "Scripture speaks about families in at least four kinds of sentence. A ",
   {
    "b": "command"
   },
   " tells you what to do: “teach them diligently to your children”. A ",
   {
    "b": "song"
   },
   " praises God and blesses the people who trust him: “Children are indeed a heritage from the LORD”. A ",
   {
    "b": "story"
   },
   " tells what happened, often without saying whether it was good: “Isaac … loved Esau; but Rebekah loved Jacob”. A ",
   {
    "b": "saying"
   },
   ", or proverb, tells you how life usually goes: “Train up a child in the way he should go”. Each kind asks something different of you. A command is to be obeyed, a song sung and trusted, a story learned from, and a proverb applied with wisdom. Reading a story as a model, or a proverb as a guarantee, has left many parents proud when they should have been thankful, and crushed when they should have been comforted."
  ],
  "selection": [
   "This is a selection, not a census. These passages were chosen because each one shows a different side of the home: teaching, telling, gift, favouritism, failure and waiting. The Bible’s sayings on children and discipline in Proverbs belong to ",
   {
    "s": "../the-wisdom-books-directions/",
    "t": "The wisdom books"
   },
   ". The instructions to children and fathers in Ephesians and Colossians are walked in ",
   {
    "s": "../faith-in-daily-life-directions/",
    "t": "Faith in daily life"
   },
   ", and the commandment to honour father and mother in ",
   {
    "s": "../the-ten-commandments-directions/",
    "t": "The Ten Commandments"
   },
   ". Marriage itself, adoption, single parenting, and caring for ageing parents have no reading of their own here. This study reads Scripture. It is not medical, legal or professional guidance."
  ],
  "close": {
   "title": "Learning the sentences of the home",
   "way": [
    "Before you hold a line about family as a promise, ask what kind of sentence it is. A command, such as ",
    {
     "r": "DEU.6.7",
     "t": "Deuteronomy 6:7"
    },
    ", is yours to obey. A song, such as ",
    {
     "r": "PSA.127",
     "t": "Psalm 127"
    },
    ", is yours to sing and trust. A story, such as Isaac’s, Jacob’s and Eli’s, is yours to learn from, and it does not always tell you what to copy. A saying, such as ",
    {
     "r": "PRO.22.6",
     "t": "Proverbs 22:6"
    },
    ", is wisdom about how things usually go, to be used with wisdom. Then ask what the passage asks of you, and what it does not promise."
   ],
   "close": [
    "The home in Scripture is not a place of perfect families. It is a place where God’s words are spoken at the table and on the road, where the old story is told when a child asks, and where a father keeps watching the road. You do not hold the house up yourself. “Unless the LORD builds the house, its builders labor in vain.”"
   ],
   "invite": [
    "If you have never trusted Christ yourself, start there before anything else in this study. The love of the father in ",
    {
     "r": "LUK.15",
     "t": "Luke 15"
    },
    " is the love of God for anyone who comes home to him. Turn from your sin and trust Jesus Christ as Lord. Read ",
    {
     "s": "../the-good-news-directions/",
     "t": "The good news: how a person is made right with God"
    },
    "."
   ],
   "question": [
    "Which words are on your heart that you want the next generation to hear, and where in an ordinary day could they hear them from you?"
   ],
   "again": [
    "Read the passage again before you answer."
   ],
   "passage": "DEU.6.4-9"
  }
